  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "If \\(x-y\\gt x\\) and \\(x+y\\lt y,\\) then:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(y\\lt x\\)",
    "B": "\\(x\\lt y\\)",
    "C": "\\(x\\lt y\\lt0\\)",
    "D": "\\(x\\lt0,\\) and \\(y\\lt0\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\lt0,\\) and \\(y\\gt0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Adding or subtracting the same expression on both sides preserves an inequality. We can therefore remove the terms that obscure the signs of the variables.\n\nAdding \\(y-x\\) to both sides of \\(x-y>x\\) gives \\(0>y\\), so \\(y<0\\). Subtracting \\(y\\) from both sides of \\(x+y<y\\) gives \\(x<0\\).\n\nThus \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are both strictly negative, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
